# Data Fetching

Next.js allows you to fetch data in different ways depending on your use case.

### Server Components (Default in App Router)
You can use `async/await` directly in your components to fetch data on the server.

```tsx
async function Page() {
  const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
  const data = await res.json();
  return <div>{data.title}</div>;
}
```

### Client-side Fetching
Use standard React hooks like `useEffect` or libraries like SWR and TanStack Query.

# Layouts & Templates

Layouts are UI that is shared between multiple pages. On navigation, layouts preserve state and remain interactive.

### Defining a Layout
```tsx
export default function DashboardLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <section>
      <nav></nav>
      {children}
    </section>
  );
}
```

# API Routes & Server Actions

### API Routes (Route Handlers)
Create custom request handlers for a given route using the Web Request and Response APIs.

```tsx
// app/api/hello/route.ts
export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return new Response('Hello, Next.js!')
}
```

### Server Actions
Asynchronous functions that are executed on the server. They can be invoked in Client and Server Components to handle form submissions and data mutations.

# Middleware

Middleware allows you to run code before a request is completed. You can then modify the response by rewriting, redirecting, modifying the request or response headers, or responding directly.

```tsx
// middleware.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export function middleware(request) {
  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/home', request.url))
}
```
